/** \addtogroup area_external */
/** \defgroup theory_external Theory of external memory
 \ingroup area_external

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/external` realize, beyond the book,
 the two-level memory of Aggarwal and Vitter and the algorithms that run in
 it, after chapters 1, Introduction, 4, List Ranking, and 5, Sorting Atomic
 Items, of Ferragina's book, i.e., the sorts by merging
 and by distribution with their lower bounds, the permutations and the
 ranking of a list. This page collects what the headers take for granted:
 the model and its notation (see \ref theory_external_notation), the cost of
 the elementary patterns of access (see \ref theory_external_costs), and,
 for each family of algorithms, the property on which its correctness rests
 and the argument behind its bound (from \ref theory_external_sort to
 \ref theory_external_lists). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on
 the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the
 chapter of Ferragina's book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_external_notation Definitions and notation

 The <em>two-level memory</em> has an internal memory of \f$ M \f$ items and
 a disk of unbounded size, divided into blocks of \f$ B \f$ consecutive
 items; an <em>I/O</em> moves one block between the disk and the memory, and
 the cost of an algorithm is the number of its I/Os, the computation in
 memory being free. An item is <em>atomic</em>: it is moved and compared
 whole, and a pair or a triple of the algorithms counts as one item. The
 model needs \f$ M \ge 2 B \f$, so that a block can be read while another is
 kept, and the algorithms of the area ask for \f$ M \ge 3 B \f$ (two inputs
 and an output) or more. `TwoLevelMemory.h` simulates it: every buffer is
 leased from a memory of \f$ M \f$ items, which a test reads back with its
 two counters of the I/Os, so that a bound of this page is checked on the
 run itself.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ M \f$, \f$ B \f$</td>
     <td>the items of the input, of the internal memory and of a block, with
         \f$ M \ge 2 B \f$ (<tt>memorySize()</tt>,
         <tt>blockSize()</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \lceil n / B \rceil \f$</td>
     <td>the blocks of a file of \f$ n \f$ items, the last one possibly
         partial (<tt>blocks()</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{scan}( n ) \f$, \f$ \mathrm{sort}( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n / B ) \f$ and
         \f$ \Theta( ( n / B ) \log_{M / B} ( n / M ) ) \f$, the I/Os of a
         scan and of a sort of \f$ n \f$ items</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ R \f$, \f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the items of a run formed in memory,
         \f$ R = \lfloor M / B \rfloor B \f$, and the fan-in of a merge or the
         number of buckets of a distribution</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \pi \f$</td>
     <td>a permutation of \f$ 0 , \ldots , n - 1 \f$, with
         \f$ \mathit{out}[ i ] = \mathit{in}[ \pi( i ) ] \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{succ}[ i ] \f$, \f$ r[ i ] \f$</td>
     <td>the successor of the item \f$ i \f$ of a list, the tail \f$ t \f$
         having \f$ \mathit{succ}[ t ] = t \f$, and its rank, the number of
         links from \f$ i \f$ to the tail</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \tilde O( \cdot ) \f$</td>
     <td>the bound up to the factor \f$ \log_{M / B} ( n / M ) \f$ of a
         sort, i.e., \f$ \tilde O( n / B ) = O( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$
         </td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_external_costs The patterns of access and their costs

 An algorithm of the internal memory that follows links (a list, a tree of
 pointers, the edges of a graph) pays up to one I/O per link on the disk,
 since two consecutive links rarely fall in one block; the algorithms of the
 area replace such links by scans and sorts, whose blocks are read whole.

 <table>
 <caption>The patterns of access of the area and their I/Os</caption>
 <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>I/Os</th><th>Realization</th></tr>
 <tr><td>scan of \f$ n \f$ items, read or written</td>
     <td>\f$ \lceil n / B \rceil \f$</td>
     <td><tt>DiskReader::next()</tt>, <tt>DiskWriter::push()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ m \f$ reads at sorted positions</td>
     <td>\f$ \min\{ m , \lceil n / B \rceil \} \f$</td>
     <td><tt>DiskReader::at()</tt> with increasing positions</td></tr>
 <tr><td>sort of \f$ n \f$ items</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( ( n / B ) \log_{M / B} ( n / M ) ) \f$</td>
     <td><tt>multiwayMergeSort()</tt>, <tt>distributionSort()</tt> (on
         average)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>permutation of \f$ n \f$ items</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( \min\{ n , \mathrm{sort}( n ) \} ) \f$</td>
     <td><tt>permuteDirect()</tt> or <tt>permuteBySorting()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>ranking of a list of \f$ n \f$ items</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$</td>
     <td><tt>externalListRank()</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 Reads at sorted positions share a block whenever two of them fall in it,
 since `DiskReader` loads a block only when it is not the one in its buffer;
 hence the coordinated scans of the algorithms (a sorted file of requests
 read together with the file that answers them) cost the scans of the two
 files, and no more.

 \section theory_external_sort Sorting

 <em>Theorem 1 (the lower bound of sorting).</em> A sort by comparisons of
 \f$ n \f$ atomic items in the two-level memory needs, for some
 \f$ t \f$ reads,
 \f[
   \binom{M}{B}^t ( B! )^{\lceil n / B \rceil} \ge n! , \quad
   \text{whence} \quad t = \Omega\Bigl( \frac{n}{B} \log_{M / B}
   \frac{n}{M} \Bigr) , \tag{1}
 \f]
 plus the \f$ \lceil n / B \rceil \f$ writes of the output (Aggarwal and
 Vitter).

 Indeed, consider the tree of the executions, each node a read and its
 children the outcomes of the read. A read brings \f$ B \f$ items into a
 memory that holds \f$ M - B \f$ others, and what it can tell is the
 positions of the new items among the old ones, at most
 \f$ \binom{M}{B} \f$ outcomes, times the \f$ B! \f$ orders of the new items
 among themselves when the block is read for the first time, which happens
 \f$ \lceil n / B \rceil \f$ times. The leaves must tell apart the
 \f$ n! \f$ orders of the input, which gives (1); taking the logarithms,
 \f$ t \log \binom{M}{B} \ge n \log ( n / B ) - O( n ) \f$ and
 \f$ \log \binom{M}{B} = O( B \log ( M / B ) ) \f$, i.e., the bound of (1).
 `sortingLowerBound()` computes the least \f$ t \f$ with the logarithms of
 the factorials, so that a test can compare it with the I/Os counted.

 A permutation is no easier, once the writes are counted too: a write
 can put its block in any of \f$ n + 1 \f$ places of the output, and the
 condition
 \f[
   ( ( n + 1 ) \binom{M}{B} )^t ( B! )^{\lceil n / B \rceil} \ge n! \tag{2}
 \f]
 gives \f$ t = \Omega( \min\{ n , \mathrm{sort}( n ) \} ) \f$
 (`permutingLowerBound()`): the minimum
 is \f$ n \f$, one read per item, only when \f$ B \f$ is smaller than the
 logarithm of the sort. Hence on the disk
 the cost of a sort is that of moving the items, not that of finding their
 order.

 The mergesort reaches (1). Its first pass forms the runs, reading
 \f$ R \f$ items at a time and sorting them in memory, which saves the
 \f$ \log_2 M \f$ lowest levels of the recursion; each further pass merges
 groups of \f$ k \f$ runs with a block of each in memory and one for the
 output, and reads and writes every block once. With
 \f$ \lceil n / R \rceil \f$ runs, the I/Os are
 \f[
   2 \lceil n / B \rceil \bigl( 1 + \lceil \log_k \lceil n / R \rceil
   \rceil \bigr) , \tag{3}
 \f]
 the value of `mergeSortIOs()`. With \f$ k = 2 \f$ (`binaryMergeSort()`)
 this is \f$ O( ( n / B ) \log_2 ( n / M ) ) \f$, and with
 \f$ k = M / B - 1 \f$ (`multiwayMergeSort()`) it is
 \f$ O( ( n / B ) \log_{M / B} ( n / M ) ) \f$, i.e., optimal by (1).
 The choice of the least head among
 \f$ k \f$ runs costs no I/O, and a tree of losers makes it with
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 k \rceil \f$ comparisons, one per level on the path of the
 winner. Replacement selection (the snow plow of Knuth) forms longer runs
 with a heap of \f$ M - 2 B \f$ items: an item larger than the last one
 written joins the current run, a smaller one the next run; on a random
 input the runs have \f$ 2 M \f$ items on average, which saves a pass when
 the number \f$ \lceil n / R \rceil \f$ of the runs of the other way lies
 between a power \f$ k^p \f$ and \f$ 2 k^p \f$, and a sorted input is one
 run.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the sample of the distribution).</em> Let the \f$ k - 1 \f$
 pivots be the items of ranks \f$ ( a + 1 ) , 2 ( a + 1 ) , \ldots \f$ of a
 sorted random sample of \f$ ( a + 1 ) k - 1 \f$ items, with
 \f$ a + 1 = \lceil 12 \ln k \rceil \f$. Then with probability at least
 \f$ 1 / 2 \f$ every bucket between two consecutive pivots has fewer than
 \f$ 4 n / k \f$ items.

 Indeed, cut the sorted input into \f$ k / 2 \f$ segments of \f$ 2 n / k \f$
 items each: a bucket with \f$ 4 n / k \f$ items or more contains a whole
 segment, and then no more than \f$ a \f$ items of the sample fall in that
 segment, while \f$ 2 ( a + 1 ) \f$ are expected there; by a bound of
 Chernoff this has probability at most \f$ e^{- ( a + 1 ) / 6} \le k^{-2} \f$,
 and the union over the \f$ k / 2 \f$ segments gives at most \f$ 1 / 2 \f$.

 Hence `distributionSort()` draws the sample again when a bucket is too
 large (at most twice), and with buckets of fewer than \f$ 4 n / k \f$ items
 the recursion has \f$ O( \log_{k / 4} ( n / M ) ) \f$ levels, each of
 which reads and writes every item once. The memory holds a block of the
 input, a block for each of the \f$ k \f$ buckets between the pivots and of
 the \f$ k - 1 \f$ buckets of the items equal to a pivot (which stop the
 recursion on repeated keys, as the three-way partition of quicksort does),
 a block of the output and the pivots; hence
 \f$ k = \lfloor ( M - B ) / ( 2 B + 1 ) \rfloor \f$, half the fan-in of the
 merge, and the same \f$ O( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$ I/Os on average.

 <table>
 <caption>The sorts of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Sort</th><th>Memory</th><th>I/Os</th><th>Remarks</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>binaryMergeSort()</tt></td><td>\f$ M \ge 3 B \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( ( n / B ) \log_2 ( n / M ) ) \f$</td>
     <td>deterministic, the logarithm in base 2</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>multiwayMergeSort()</tt></td><td>\f$ M \ge 3 B \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$</td>
     <td>deterministic, optimal; fewer runs with the snow plow</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>distributionSort()</tt></td><td>\f$ M \ge 5 B + 2 \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$ on average</td>
     <td>randomized, fan-in about half that of the merge</td></tr>
 </table>

 On \f$ D \f$ disks read in parallel, the striping (the \f$ D \f$ disks as
 one with blocks of \f$ D B \f$ items) turns either sort into one of
 \f$ O( ( n / D B ) \log_{M / D B} ( n / M ) ) \f$ I/Os, which loses a
 factor \f$ 1 - \log_{M / B} D \f$ in the base of the logarithm; Greed Sort of
 Nodine and Vitter recovers the optimum. The functions of the area simulate
 one disk.

 \section theory_external_permute Permuting

 `permuteDirect()` reads \f$ \mathit{in}[ \pi( i ) ] \f$ for
 \f$ i = 0 , 1 , \ldots \f$ and writes the output by a scan: at most
 \f$ n \f$ reads, fewer when consecutive positions fall in one block.
 `permuteBySorting()` instead sorts the pairs \f$ \langle i , \pi( i )
 \rangle \f$ by \f$ \pi( i ) \f$, fills in \f$ \mathit{in}[ \pi( i ) ] \f$
 by one scan of the input, which is now read in order, and sorts the triples
 back by \f$ i \f$: two sorts and three scans, \f$ O( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$;
 by (2) the better of the two is optimal, the direct one only when
 \f$ B \f$ is below the logarithm of the sort.

 \section theory_external_lists Ranking a list

 In memory a list is ranked in \f$ O( n ) \f$ time by a walk back from the
 tail (`listRank()`), which on the disk costs up to one I/O per item. The
 algorithms of the area avoid the walk in two ways, both from the parallel
 algorithms.

 <em>Lemma 3 (pointer jumping).</em> Start from \f$ s[ i ] =
 \mathit{succ}[ i ] \f$, and \f$ r[ i ] = 1 \f$ but at the tail, where it is
 0; if each round sets, for all the items at once,
 \f[
   r[ i ] \gets r[ i ] + r[ s[ i ] ] , \qquad s[ i ] \gets s[ s[ i ] ] ,
   \tag{4}
 \f]
 then after \f$ j \f$ rounds \f$ r[ i ] \f$ is the number of links from
 \f$ i \f$ to \f$ s[ i ] \f$, which is \f$ \min\{ 2^j , r( i ) \} \f$ away
 (Wyllie).

 Indeed, (4) adds the length of the jump of \f$ s[ i ] \f$ to that of
 \f$ i \f$, and by induction on \f$ j \f$ both are \f$ 2^{j-1} \f$ unless the
 tail is reached. Hence \f$ \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$ rounds rank the list,
 with \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ operations (`listRankPointerJumping()`). A round
 of (4) is a parallel step
 \f$ A[ a_i ] \gets \mathrm{op}( A[ a_i ] , A[ b_i ] ) \f$, and
 `parallelStep()` makes it on the disk by sorting the requests by
 \f$ b_i \f$, answering them by one scan of \f$ A \f$, sorting them back by
 \f$ a_i \f$ and applying them by a second scan; thus any parallel
 algorithm of \f$ T \f$ steps on \f$ n \f$ processors costs
 \f$ \tilde O( ( n / B ) T ) \f$ I/Os, and `externalPointerJumping()`
 \f$ \tilde O( ( n / B ) \log n ) \f$.

 The logarithm goes away by recursion. If \f$ I \f$ is an
 <em>independent set</em>, i.e., no item of \f$ I \f$ is the successor of
 another item of \f$ I \f$ nor the tail, the items whose successor is in
 \f$ I \f$ can jump over it, adding its rank to their own; the remaining
 list is ranked recursively, and each item of \f$ I \f$ then gets the rank
 of its successor plus its own. With \f$ | I | \ge n / 8 \f$ and each level
 made of a constant number of sorts and scans (the joins of each record
 with the record of its successor),
 \f[
   T( n ) = \tilde O( n / B ) + T( 7 n / 8 ) = \tilde O( n / B ) , \tag{5}
 \f]
 since the sizes of the levels decrease geometrically
 (`externalListRank()`). The independent
 sets come in two kinds.

 <table>
 <caption>The independent sets of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Set</th><th>Size</th><th>Rounds</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>randomIndependentSet()</tt>: a coin per item, the heads whose
         successor shows tails</td>
     <td>\f$ n / 4 \f$ on average, at least \f$ n / 8 \f$ after
         \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ attempts on average</td>
     <td>1</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>independentSetOfColors()</tt> on
         <tt>deterministicCoinTossing()</tt>: the local minima of three
         colors</td>
     <td>at least about \f$ n / 4 \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log^* n ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 An item enters the random set with probability \f$ 1 / 4 \f$ (its own coin
 heads, that of its successor tails), and two items of the set cannot be
 consecutive, since the successor of one shows tails. The deterministic
 coin tossing of Cole and Vishkin starts from the colors
 \f$ c[ i ] = i \f$, which differ on adjacent items, and replaces at each
 round \f$ c[ i ] \f$ by \f$ 2 \pi + z \f$, with \f$ \pi \f$ the lowest bit
 in which \f$ c[ i ] \f$ and \f$ c[ \mathit{succ}[ i ] ] \f$ differ and
 \f$ z \f$ the value of that bit in \f$ c[ i ] \f$.

 <em>Lemma 4 (deterministic coin tossing).</em> If adjacent colors differ
 before a round, they differ after it, and colors of \f$ b \f$ bits become
 colors of \f$ \lceil \log_2 b \rceil + 1 \f$ bits.

 Indeed, if \f$ i \f$ and its successor \f$ j \f$ get the same new color,
 they have the same \f$ \pi \f$ and the same bit \f$ \pi \f$; but
 \f$ \pi \f$ is a bit in which the old colors of \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$
 differ, a contradiction. The new color is at most \f$ 2 ( b - 1 ) + 1 \f$,
 which has \f$ \lceil \log_2 b \rceil + 1 \f$ bits. Hence after
 \f$ O( \log^* n ) \f$ rounds the colors are in \f$ \{ 0 , \ldots , 5 \} \f$,
 and the items of colors 5, 4 and 3 in turn take the least color of
 \f$ \{ 0 , 1 , 2 \} \f$ that neither neighbour has, which keeps the colors
 proper since the items of one color are not adjacent. The local minima of
 three colors are independent, and between two consecutive local minima
 there are at most 3 other items. `listRankIndependentSet()` runs the
 recursion in memory with either set; on the disk the deterministic sets
 would repeat the joins of their \f$ O( \log^* n ) \f$ rounds at every
 level, and the area uses them only in memory. The optimal parallel
 rankings of Cole and Vishkin and of Anderson and Miller use the same sets
 to reach \f$ O( n ) \f$ operations in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ time, and on the
 disk \f$ \Theta( \mathrm{sort}( n ) ) \f$, the bound of (5), is optimal
 (Chiang et al.).

